Hi Hafiz,

This is likely related to a process called "spatial confounding" in the statistics literature. In short, the covariates could potentially be correlated with the estimated spatial random effect, which makes them harder to estimate separately from each other (and harder to converge). You will likely need to run a model longer to ensure convergence of a spatial model as opposed to a non-spatial model, so it shouldn't come as a surprise if you need to run the spatial model longer. There are mixed perspectives among statisticians on how to best "deal" with spatial confounding. A good introduction to the concept in terms of species distribution modeling can be found here <https://nsojournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/ecog.06183>, while some more recent perspectives on spatial confounding are here  <https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00031305.2021.1946149#abstract>and here <https://academic.oup.com/biomet/article/112/2/asae070/7930673?guestAccessKey=>.

Jeff

